---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How many traces and spans a sampling rate produces, how much trace data that is a day, and how often a rare event still shows up in the sample. A teaching model with round numbers, not Google's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Requests per second (thousands)" name="r" min="1" max="1000" step="1" value="100" />
      <RangeField label="Spans in one request (one per call)" name="s" min="2" max="200" step="2" value="40" />
      <RangeField label="Trace 1 request in" name="n" min="1" max="10000" step="1" value="1024" />
      <RangeField label="Bytes per span" name="b" min="100" max="1000" step="10" value="430" />
      <RangeField label="Days of trace data kept" name="d" min="1" max="30" step="1" value="7" />
      <RangeField label="A rare event happens in 1 request out of (thousands)" name="e" min="1" max="1000" step="1" value="100" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Google's system or data. From the Dapper report: spans average 426 bytes in the repository, sampling rates as low as 1 in 1,024 gave enough data for high-volume services, and the report's argument is that a notable pattern that appears once will appear thousands of times in a high-traffic service. The request rate, the number of spans in a request, the retention and the rare event are my guesses that you can change. Assumptions: every request has the same number of spans, requests are spread evenly through the day, a day has 86,400 seconds, and the decision to trace is random with the probability you set.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 9; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['r', 's', 'n', 'b', 'd', 'e'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const rps = val('[data-r]') * 1000; const spans = val('[data-s]'); const oneIn = val('[data-n]'); const bytes = val('[data-b]');
      const days = val('[data-d]'); const rare = val('[data-e]') * 1000;
      const traces = rps / oneIn; const spanRate = traces * spans; const perDayGb = (spanRate * bytes * 86400) / 1e9;
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Traces recorded per second', f1(traces)],
        ['Spans written per second', f1(spanRate)],
        ['Spans per second if every request were traced', f1(rps * spans)],
        ['Trace data per second (MB)', f1((spanRate * bytes) / 1e6)],
        ['Trace data per day (GB)', f1(perDayGb)],
        ['Trace data kept (TB)', f1((perDayGb * days) / 1000)],
        ['Data saved by sampling, compared with tracing everything', `${f1(oneIn)} times`],
        ['Rare events per day in all traffic', f1((rps * 86400) / rare)],
        ['Rare events that land in a trace each day', f1((rps * 86400) / rare / oneIn)],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
